the winners of the 2021 nobel peace  prize have just been announced in oslo  the award was given to two journalists  maria ressa from the philippines and  dimitri muritov from russia the nobel  committee cited their efforts to  safeguard freedom of expression in their  respective countries ressa founded the  investigative journalism website rappler  and has focused much of her work on  philippine president rodrigo duterte's  controversial and violent war on drugs  muritov was one of the founders of  russia's independent newspaper navia  gazetta which has a fundamentally  critical attitude towards power the  committee said and has been its  editor-in-chief since 1995.  the norwegian nobel committee  has decided  to award the nobel peace prize for 2021  to  maria rissa  and dimitri muratov for their efforts  to safeguard freedom of expression  which is a precondition  for democracy and lasting  peace ms ressa and mr muratov  are receiving the peace prize  for their courageous fight  for freedom of expression in the  philippines  and in russia  speaking by telephone right after the  announcement maria said she was in shock  and explained what impact the prize will  have on her and her team  it means we keep doing what we do right  if this is added energy added focus i  mean the the biggest problem that we  have is that  so much happens in darkness and i think  what we've always tried to do is to  continue doing journalism accountability  journalism  investigations that will hold power to  account it's just become far more  difficult  with the advent of technology and social  media and the way social media has  allowed  budding authoritarians and dictators to  be to exploit their algorithms and  and take power and crumble democracies  from within so i mean thank you so much  for recognizing  what effort it takes to do the job of a  journalist today  well for more i'm joined in the studio  by dw's political correspondent simon  young simon thanks for coming in firstly  let's start with tell us a little bit  more about maria ressa and who she is  and rappler in particular yeah  the filipino journalist maria wrestler  she's  really been a leader of uh media  scrutiny of president duterte in  particular  and his regime she's  been in a sort of a battle with him  really maria ressa criticizes president  duterte as an authoritarian who's  undermined democracy  in that country and she says that the  country's become a dictatorship uh  disguised as a democracy so uh she's  been pursuing uh his career since uh  before he became president back in 2015  she interviewed duterte and  um it was in in that interview for  instance that he revealed that he  personally had had killed three people  and that's  been one of the hallmarks of his  campaign against uh drugs in the  philippines uh which she says has gone  way too far and as i say is undermining  democracy her platform this uh online  magazine rappler  is a special um  media product because it is run by women  and uh  it's been repeatedly in uh conflict with  the duterte regime there's been uh more  than a dozen uh prosecutions against uh  rappler and its journalists since 2017  and maria ressa herself was uh has been  prosecuted  over a text that appeared on that online  site it was an  expose of corruption by a senior judge  in the philippines so it just shows you  across a whole range of of levels and  different stories and for for many years  maria ressa has been sort of leading the  fight to bring uh the leaders of the of  of her country to account all right in a  moment we get across to russia and speak  further on dimitri muritov so i won't  ask you about him right now but what  message do you think the the nobel  academy are trying to send with these  awards that they've handed out today  well as was said in the citation what  they uh are intending to do is to  underscore the basic human rights uh two  in particular uh the freedom of  expression and the freedom of the media  and they say that their logic is that uh  those freedoms are essential to  preserving and promoting international  peace dialogue between nations and  within nations of course to bring  stability  uh and so they hope that by putting the  spotlight on these two leading uh  opponents it has to be said  media representatives who who stand up  to authoritarian regimes in the  philippines and in russia they hope that  to bolster uh not just the media in  those countries but i think in other  places around the world where conditions  are difficult for journalists indeed all  right simon young thank you very much  and we can speak now to emily show and  she's our correspondent in moscow emily  thanks for joining us tell us a bit more  about the newspaper novella gazetta and  it's editor in chief and nobel peace  prize winner dmitry muratov  well dmitry muratov is one of the  founding members of nova gazette and  he's actually been its editor in chief  as well for over 20 years  and i think an important figure in  standing up to  the government here  or rather criticizing it through  journalism and also standing up for  freedom of speech here in russia now  nova gazette itself is somewhat of an  institution in the country um it's a  newspaper that comes out three times a  week and they're really known for their  investigative journalism  including particularly into human rights  issues and also  corruption for example  it was one of the newspapers one of the  media outlets that revealed the presence  of russian soldiers in eastern ukraine  during the war there  and also the newspaper that revealed the  fact that gay people in chechnya in the  russian region of chechnya were being  tortured for their sexuality murataf and  the other journalists at nova gazetta  have been really uncompromising over the  years in upholding their values  and continuing their investigative work  despite the increasing difficulties  they've received a lot of threats over  the years one journalist that i spoke to  in my reporting there about the  newspaper  told me that it sometimes feels like  they're working in a war zone or you  know walking over a minefield because  actually six of the journalists from  that newspaper have been killed over the  years for their work including the  well-known investigative journalist  annapolitovsky  actually it was the 15th anniversary of  her murder yesterday  and we went to speak to some of her  colleagues at the newspaper  let's take a look at our report  journalist yelina kastuchinko is having  a first look at what used to be the  office of anna paletkovskaya her  colleague shown here in archive footage  now it's a museum in her honor  kastuchinka says palitkovskaya's  in-depth reporting about the wars in  chechnya was the reason she came to work  at the paper novea gazetta as an intern  at the time anna palitkovskaya was still  alive  she wrote about topics that no one else  wrote about because they were too scared  and she wrote in a way that meant it was  impossible to remain indifferent  her words hit you right in your soul and  they stayed there  is an investigative journalist herself  she often writes about controversial  social and political topics like lgbtq  rights and the presence of russian  soldiers in eastern ukraine she says  palitkovskaya is her role model even now  i constantly keep her in my head as an  example while i'm writing all the time  of course i can hardly hope that i'll  grow to her level that's impossible but  it's as if she lights my way  novaya gazetta is one of the few  remaining critical media outlets in  russia over the years six of the  newspapers journalists were killed for  their work including anapalikovskaya  many in the team still remember her huge  dedication  once she came into the canteen while we  were having lunch and started saying the  chechens are torturing people and  everyone just said wait a minute let's  have some lunch first she was always  thinking about her work  there were always lines of people  outside her office people who had come  from the caucasus from chechnya to ask  for her help  we can't ever betray our journalistic  standards because that would also mean  betraying the people who gave their life  for honest journalism we here continue  their work  was renowned for her reporting about  russia's wars in chechnya and her fierce  criticism of the kremlin's policies  on october 7 2006 she was shot four  times at her moscow apartment  those who carried out the killing were  later sentenced in court but those who  ordered it were never held accountable  15 years after the murder of  annapolitkowska pressure on independent  journalists here in russia has been  rising in recent months media outlets  and journalists critical of the kremlin  have faced police searches and criminal  cases and many have been declared  foreign agents  the foreign agent label shows that the  russian government sees journalists as  enemies elena kastuccinca feels the  reporter says she regularly receives  threats for her work but to her anna  palitkovskaya's example shows that  journalists must carry on even in the  face of intimidation oluje  people who kill journalists or think  about it cannot be allowed to be given  the illusion that their actions will  stop a certain topic being covered that  it could get them off the hook it won't  when a journalist is killed that  person's colleagues must step up and  take their place  office will remain empty but her  fearlessness continues to inspire those  who refuse to be silent  inspiring those that refuse to be silent  indeed and that's obviously what this  award today was trying to uh say but the  award is ju is about more than just one  newspaper emily it's also about freedom  of press in russia as a whole which has  come under severe threat in recent years  tell us more  well i would say that nova gazette is  becoming  you know a real rarity  now on the media landscape in russia  there's been increasing pressure on  independent journalists you know over  the last few years but i would say this  last year in particular the pressure has  really been upped  we've seen a whole list of media outlets  um that are critical of the kremlin  being declared uh to be so-called  foreign agents which means that they  face you know not only the stigma that  comes with that label but also a huge  administrative burden which can you know  have mean problems with financing and so  on some media outlets have even had to  close and also individual journalists  have been declared uh foreign agents as  well some of the people that i spoke to  about that have told me that it really  makes them feel like they are enemies in  their own country and even in recent  months there's been a hashtag that's  been trending  which is journalism is not a crime  several independent journalists have had  to leave the country there have been  criminal cases against them police  searches and so on so i think this is a  really important signal right now for  journalists um here in russia  to keep doing uh what they are doing you  know from the international community  indeed an important signal all right  emily thank you very much dw's emily  shown in moscow
 

